10:46:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: “Survival of the fittest” does not mean “kill every weaker rival.” In evolutionary biology, **fitness** means leaving more viable offspring in a given environment. Killing a rival can sometimes increase fitness, but so can making him retreat, holding a territory, being chosen by females, surviving winter, avoiding injury, or successfully raising offspring. For many animals, a fight to the death is a bad strategy because: - the winner can be badly injured; - wounds may become infected; - an injured animal may not hunt, flee, or mate afterward; - the loser can often leave and compete elsewhere; - displays and short fights can establish dominance at far lower risk. That is why evolution often produces ways to **settle contests without lethal escalation**: deer displays and antler wrestling, bird song and territorial displays, elephant-seal vocal contests, primate threat displays, and so on. Lethal fighting is favored only when its expected reproductive payoff outweighs its high costs—for example, in certain unusually intense contests, infanticide during some male takeovers, or attacks where escape is impossible. It is not the universal “point” of competition. Also, “weaker males” are not necessarily genetically inferior in a broad sense. A smaller male may survive better in another habitat, reproduce later, use a different mating strategy, or carry traits beneficial under changing conditions. Evolution has no plan to purify a species; it is simply differential reproduction under current conditions.
