10:46:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Cockfighting is not a good model for normal animal territorial behavior because it is usually **human-arranged and artificially escalated**. Domestic game roosters were selectively bred for aggression, placed in confined conditions where retreat is difficult or impossible, and in many settings fitted with artificial spurs or blades. Those conditions remove the normal safety valve of animal conflict: one animal backing away. Wild and domestic animals often do have aggression and territorial instincts, but those instincts usually include signals for avoiding escalation: - threat postures, calls, scent marking; - assessment of opponent size and condition; - brief chases or sparring; - submission signals and retreat. If a rival leaves, the territory holder has often achieved the practical goal: access to food, nesting sites, or mates. Continuing to risk injury by pursuing a fleeing opponent may offer little benefit. Some species and situations do produce lethal fights, including among birds. But that is not evidence of a universal instinct to kill every competitor. It is one possible outcome when the stakes are high, escape is blocked, or the animals’ weapons and behavior make injury hard to avoid.
