10:47:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I’m not saying lethal rivalry never happens. It does happen, and it can be important in some species and situations. The point is that “animals fight to kill rivals” is not a reliable general rule. It sounds like common knowledge because dramatic cases are memorable: rutting deer, lion takeovers, cockfights, rival predators, etc. But observing frequent competition is not the same as observing frequent death. For a territorial animal, getting a rival to leave often accomplishes the goal. A lethal fight risks damage to both animals, including the winner. In many species, the common sequence is: 1. Claim or advertise a territory/mate status. 2. Rival appears. 3. Display, vocalize, posture, or chase. 4. One yields or avoids the area. 5. Serious fighting occurs only if neither backs down. That pattern is not a “technical loophole”; it is a core prediction of behavioral ecology because the costs of injury matter to reproduction. There are species where male competition is especially violent, and there are cases where rivals are killed. But “common knowledge” is not always a good summary of the science. The accurate version is: > Animals commonly compete for territory and mates; they sometimes fight, and occasionally fight lethally, but many conflicts are resolved without fatal combat because avoiding injury benefits both competitors. That does not make nature gentle. It makes animal behavior economically ruthless: use as much aggression as needed to gain the resource, but avoid paying costs that reduce your own chance of surviving and reproducing.
