10:47:22 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=2106 miss=364
10:47:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Neither extreme is right. Animals **can** fight with behavior that is capable of killing a rival, and in some circumstances they may persist in an attack that kills. Rival males can inflict fatal wounds over territory, rank, food, or access to mates. That is real biology, not merely documentary drama. But it is also not true that every—or even most—rivalry is an intentional fight to the death. In many species, the usual outcome is avoidance, display, a short clash, or one animal retreating. Those behaviors are also genuine competition and can determine who gets mates. Your teacher’s statement was likely a simplified classroom version of sexual selection: males may compete, stronger or better-adapted competitors may often gain more mating opportunities, and traits involved in competition can evolve. The misleading part would be treating that as a universal rule that animals always seek to kill weaker males to remove their genes. Nature documentaries generally show real events, but they select unusual, visually compelling moments. They are not a random sample of all the quiet encounters that ended with a call, display, chase, or retreat. So the accurate answer is: - **Yes:** lethal rival conflict exists and sometimes matters evolutionarily. - **No:** lethal combat is not the default purpose or outcome of animal competition across animals. - **And:** “fitness” means reproductive success overall, not simply being the strongest fighter or killing competitors.
