10:47:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: “Kill or be killed” captures that nature can be harsh, but it is a slogan, not a general description of animal behavior. Animals do not need a human-like conscious plan to calculate costs and benefits. Natural selection can favor instincts such as: - threaten first; - fight if challenged; - stop when the opponent submits or flees; - avoid a larger rival; - defend offspring more fiercely than a feeding site. Those are still “basic instincts.” Displays and retreat are not the opposite of instinct; they are evolved parts of how many animals avoid needless injury. If two rivals fight, the outcome is often not “one kills or dies,” but “one gets control of the territory and the other leaves.” That can be enough for the winner to reproduce, while both avoid wounds that could later kill them. When neither can retreat or the stakes are unusually high, conflict may escalate and death can result. So the simple version is: nature favors animals that successfully reproduce. Sometimes that involves killing; very often it involves intimidating, outlasting, displacing, or avoiding a rival instead.
