10:47:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your grandpa’s observations may have been sincere, and bucks absolutely do fight hard during the rut. They can gore each other, break bones, become exhausted, or die from injuries; antlers can also become locked together, leaving both animals unable to feed or escape predators. But among deer, death is usually not the normal endpoint of a contest. Most buck interactions involve scent marking, parallel walking, posturing, sparring, chasing, or one animal yielding. Even antler fights often end when one disengages. The spectacular or tragic cases are the ones hunters remember and retell because they are unusual. There is also a difference between: - **“Bucks may fight so violently that one can die”** — true. - **“Bucks normally fight with the goal of killing one another”** — not generally supported. - **“Only the strongest buck ever breeds”** — also not true; mating success varies with age, timing, local conditions, female choice, alternative strategies, and survival. Traditional firsthand knowledge can be valuable, but memorable field stories are not the same as a complete record of all encounters—including the many that end quietly before anyone sees them.
