10:48:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Because the difference matters: “animals sometimes fight lethally” is ordinary, accurate common knowledge; “animals generally fight rivals to the death because evolution eliminates weaker genes” is a misleading claim about animal behavior and evolution. It is not nitpicking to distinguish them. That simplified story can lead to several false conclusions: - that violent escalation is the normal outcome rather than one possible outcome; - that retreat or submission means an animal is not competing; - that evolution has a purpose of removing “weak” genes; - that the winner of a fight is necessarily genetically superior overall. Your underlying point—that wild animals can be brutally violent and may kill rivals—is correct. The scientific qualification is simply that this is not the only, or usually the default, way competition works. Both ideas can be held at once: - Nature includes real lethal conflict. - Natural selection also favors avoiding unnecessary injury, so many contests end before lethal force is used. That is not denying common knowledge; it is making the common shorthand more accurate.
