10:49:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: People with long experience hunting, ranching, or watching wildlife often have valuable observations: animals can be extremely violent, rival males do injure one another, and sometimes one kills another. That is not in dispute. But firsthand experience also naturally emphasizes the memorable encounters: a dead buck with locked antlers, a mauling, a bloody dominance fight. The far more frequent non-events—one animal scent-marking, avoiding another, displaying briefly, or retreating—are easy to miss because nothing dramatic happens. This is not an “academic versus regular people” issue. Hunters and field biologists often agree on the basic practical point: animals fight when necessary, but avoid costly injury when they can. A hunter may see a buck drive another away; a biologist may call that a successful territorial contest. They are describing the same behavior in different language. The reasonable middle ground is: - Wild animals are not harmless or “polite.” - Serious and lethal fights happen. - Killing a rival is not the inevitable or universal goal of every competition, because an injured winner can lose just as badly in the longer term. That is a description of what animals do, not a dismissal of people who have seen it.
