10:50:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That concern is valid. People should never interpret “many animals avoid escalation” as “wild animals are safe” or “they are bluffing.” A wild animal can switch from display to attack very quickly, especially if it is cornered, protecting young, defending food, in breeding season, injured, habituated to people, or unable to retreat. Even an animal that is only trying to drive someone away can cause catastrophic injury because of its size, teeth, claws, antlers, hooves, or bite force. For human safety, the practical rule is simple: - Keep substantial distance. - Do not approach, feed, corner, touch, or get between an animal and its young/food/escape route. - Treat warning signs—staring, vocalizing, raised fur, ears back, pawing, charging, head lowering, bluff charges—as serious. - Follow local wildlife guidance for the species and area. Whether an animal’s internal “intent” is to kill, defend, intimidate, or escape is not something a person should gamble on. The safe assumption is: **it is capable of seriously injuring or killing you, and you should give it space.**
