10:51:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: “Nature, red in tooth and claw” is a famous poetic phrase from Alfred, Lord Tennyson’s *In Memoriam* (1850). It captures the real violence, predation, suffering, and competition seen in nature. It has stuck around because it is vivid and often feels true. But it is a metaphor, not a zoological rule. “Tooth and claw” especially evokes predators killing prey—which is indeed a central part of many food webs. It does not establish that animals generally kill every same-species rival in territorial or mating disputes. A plain summary is: - Nature includes animals killing other animals, including sometimes their own species. - Wild-animal fights can be vicious and deadly. - Yet many rival encounters still end through avoidance, threats, submission, or displacement rather than death. The phrase is compatible with all of that. It conveys that nature is not gentle; it does not tell us the frequency or purpose of every kind of animal conflict.
