10:51:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A high death rate among young male lions would show that male competition is dangerous, but it would not by itself prove that every territorial fight has the purpose of killing. Young males face several overlapping risks: - being expelled from their natal pride and forced to roam; - hunger and poor hunting success while nomadic; - injuries from clashes with resident males; - attacks by groups of males, not just one-on-one fights; - vulnerability after injury; - disease, parasites, and predation in weakened animals. In lions, male coalitions sometimes do attack intruding males with potentially lethal force, especially in pride takeovers or severe territorial conflict. So lethal intent can absolutely be part of the behavior in those events. But a population-level mortality statistic cannot tell you whether a particular death came from an immediate kill, later infection, starvation after displacement, or another consequence of losing status or territory. The best conclusion is: **young male lions often face very high risk from male competition, and some encounters are lethal.** That supports the view that rivalry is serious—not that killing is the sole purpose or usual endpoint of every contest.
