10:51:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It can be emotionally satisfying because it is a clear, dramatic story—and it captures something real: nature can be indifferent, harsh, and lethal, and animals that cannot survive or reproduce often leave fewer descendants. But “the strongest eliminate the weak” is still a metaphor, not the whole mechanism. In biology, the animals that leave offspring are not always the biggest or most violent. Sometimes they are the ones that avoid injury, find food efficiently, cooperate, hide well, resist disease, reproduce at the right time, care for young, or simply survive a changing environment. A rival that retreats is not necessarily “eliminated”; it may breed elsewhere or later. The more honest version is arguably not cleaner, but it is closer to reality: > Nature has no fairness rule and no goal of rewarding strength. Traits spread when, in a particular setting, they help their carriers leave more offspring. That process can include brutal lethal fights. It also includes avoidance, chance, cooperation, disease, parental care, and environmental change. The gray areas are not a softening of nature; they are part of its rawness.
