10:52:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Male betta fish are a good example of animals that can be highly aggressive toward same-sex rivals. You generally should **not house two adult male bettas together** in a typical aquarium: they may flare, chase, bite, shred fins, cause severe stress, and one may eventually die from wounds, stress, or inability to feed. But even this example does not show that all animal rivalry is literally “fight until one is dead.” In the wild, male bettas can establish small territories and use displays—flaring fins, spreading gills, color changes, and chasing—to make another male leave. In an aquarium, the fish cannot reliably create enough distance or leave the other’s territory. Confinement turns an encounter that might have ended in displacement into repeated contact and chronic aggression. So the practical conclusion is uncompromising: **do not put two male bettas in the same tank unless you have specialized, expert-managed conditions designed to prevent contact.** They are capable of killing or fatally stressing each other. The biological conclusion remains: bettas demonstrate that lethal same-species aggression is real; they do not establish that death is the normal aim or outcome of rival encounters across all animals.
